
A `var` declared in a window body is that window's state, the way a
component's `var` is the component's. The checker leaves it as a top-level
LocalVar in the body and passHoistState is what makes the two the same shape;
before that, no target implemented the shape at all.

It only looked implemented on the four non-html targets, because the
initializer still appeared in the output -- as a local of the render function,
reset every frame, assigned from a model field nothing declared. bubbletea
emitted Go that does not compile, android Kotlin naming an undeclared `n`,
and html emitted no state and no binding whatsoever.

So what to read in the golden is the *qualified* access -- `state.n`, `m.n`,
the remembered `n` -- and not the initializer: an unqualified `n` is exactly
what each of these looked like when it was broken, which is what the two
denies say. The same program written as a component is in
window_state_comp.txtar, and every target here has to produce the same shape
for both.

deny out/none/html/index.html `= n \+ 1` -- the click handler assigns through state.n; a bare `n` is the unbound local the broken output wrote
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `(?m)^\tn = ` -- the counter is a model field assigned as m.n, not a render-function local reset every frame
-- win.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

window #home(title="Home") {
    var n = 0
    vbox {
        text(value="{n}")
        button #inc(text="+", @click { n = n + 1 })
    }
}
